WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.888 --> 00:00:12.830
PAUL PETERSON: I have with me today Jeb
Bush, the former governor of Florida,

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:15.410
who when he was governor
saw the greatest

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:19.340
improvement in student performance
of any state in the United States.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:24.620
That was between 1998 and 2006.

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:27.230
And one of the things
that he did as governor

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:31.980
was to address the problem
of teacher quality.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:36.380
Jeb, there's a lot of problems
in American education today.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:39.230
And one of them is the
declining quality of teachers.

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:42.500
It used to be that we
recruited a lot of teachers

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.880
from the very best
schools in the country.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.850
It's harder to do that today.

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:50.390
There is Teach for America, which
is bringing a lot of talented people

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:51.880
into the teaching profession.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:57.620
But in general, it's very difficult
to get our very best college graduates

00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:59.000
to go into teaching.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.920
So what were some of the
things that you did in Florida,

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:06.050
and what are some of the things
that could be done to enhance

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:07.676
the quality of the teaching profession?

00:01:07.676 --> 00:01:08.924
JEB BUSH: Well thank you Paul.

00:01:08.924 --> 00:01:10.340
It's always a joy to be with you.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:14.510
You're one of the experts that
takes data and analyzes it

00:01:14.510 --> 00:01:16.220
and does the kind of
research that allows

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:18.320
policymakers to implement policies.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.540
And that's what I got to do as governor.

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:26.819
In Florida we had a growing student
population and a teaching cadre

00:01:26.819 --> 00:01:28.610
that was diverse of
course, because Florida

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:31.430
is a diverse state,
but a lot of teachers

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:33.590
that were nearing the
end of their career,

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:35.790
because of just the
aging of our population.

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:41.480
So 60,000 teachers-- students a year,
combining with a declining teacher

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:44.790
population, required us to
act before other states.

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:46.790
PAUL PETERSON: So more
students, fewer teachers.

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:48.123
JEB BUSH: Yeah, not a good plan.

00:01:48.123 --> 00:01:49.980
That was not a good plan at all.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:50.730
So what did we do?

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:52.100
Well we opened up our system.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:55.700
We have these antiquated
certification programs

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:59.270
that exist in the country that were
designed literally a hundred years ago.

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:03.600
And the schools of education had
robust programs back in the day.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.060
But they've de-emphasized that.

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:08.900
And so we've created
reciprocity for anybody

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:12.620
that is credentialed as a teacher in
any state can come teach in Florida.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:16.379
PAUL PETERSON: And that was one
of the real obstacles to teachers

00:02:16.379 --> 00:02:18.920
who had to move from one state
to another and all of a sudden

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:20.670
lost their job and
couldn't get them back.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:24.440
JEB BUSH: The system was designed
to protect the existing teachers.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:27.590
It was a protectionist
system and we opened it up.

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:29.660
I think other things
that are important to do

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:34.220
is if you're a subject matter
expert, if you're an engineer

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:37.880
and you want to teach math, in
the old days you couldn't do that.

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:39.110
You had to go back to school.

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:41.300
PAUL PETERSON: Well that's just
exactly where the shortages are,

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:42.400
in math and science.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:43.316
JEB BUSH: In the STEM.

00:02:43.316 --> 00:02:46.160
So in Florida if you're
a subject matter expert,

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:50.810
you can get a temporary
certification to teach.

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:55.550
And we've streamline the process to be
able to get a permanent certification.

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:58.850
Paying more for those for the
places where you have shortages

00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:02.540
is part of this, and paying
people, paying teachers

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:04.980
for rising student achievement.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.810
So if you want to build a
professional teaching cadre,

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:10.850
you've got to have
professional development.

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:14.462
But you also have to pay
the extraordinary teachers

00:03:14.462 --> 00:03:15.920
more so they stay in the classroom.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.530
PAUL PETERSON: But we tend to
have a lock step teaching--

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:19.544
JEB BUSH: Exactly.

00:03:19.544 --> 00:03:20.960
PAUL PETERSON: --payment schedule.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:23.540
JEB BUSH: And so Washington,
DC is the best example

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:27.050
of this where teachers could opt out of
the traditional collective bargaining

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:30.200
process, which is a step plan
based on how long you were there,

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:35.090
to dramatic increases in spending
for teachers that had great peer

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:37.820
reviews by their fellow
teachers, by the principal who's

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:42.230
the CEO of the school of course,
and by test scores for students.

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:45.530
And the implementation of test
scores as part of this I think

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:47.300
is a central part of this.

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:48.920
Now in Florida we had something else.

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:52.250
We bonus teachers outside
of the collective bargaining

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:56.780
agreement for schools that show
improvement or are A rated.

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:00.710
They get, in effect, $100
per student the school does,

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:03.640
and that goes back in the form of
a bonus to the school personnel.

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:05.960
It's $150 million a year.

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:11.450
In Florida if a teacher
begins to teach AP classes,

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:15.140
the school gets rewarded with
higher points for a higher grade.

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:19.149
And the teacher gets $50 per
student for students that pass.

00:04:19.149 --> 00:04:21.740
PAUL PETERSON: Well does that
have any effect on your AP--

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:22.400
JEB BUSH: Oh my gosh.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:24.191
PAUL PETERSON:
--enrollment, participation?

00:04:24.191 --> 00:04:27.110
JEB BUSH: What a surprise,
we've had three digit percentage

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:31.130
increases, particularly in the
schools that never had AP classes.

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:34.550
So the prime beneficiaries
have been low income students.

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:38.690
And teachers get on average, you know,
if they're really all in on this,

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:40.160
they get up to $3,000.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:42.280
Similarly for career certification.

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:46.520
So rewarding teachers for
dealing with the things you want,

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:51.650
which is making sure that students
are able to take advanced courses,

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:53.990
and dealing with the
shortages as it relates

00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:58.340
to effective teachers in low
performing schools, teachers

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:01.580
in the STEM-related areas, and
market orientation in essence

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:03.290
is what has to be done.

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:04.830
And Florida began that process.

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:09.830
So look, if teachers do a good job, you
don't want them to leave the classroom.

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:12.760
You want to reward them,
but not for inputs, not

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:14.570
for being there for a long time.

00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:17.070
There are some great teachers
that have been there 30 years,

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:19.150
but there's also some
that aren't that good.

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:22.810
Why not reward people for
rising student achievement.

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:25.780
PAUL PETERSON: Is there any way to
discourage the ineffective teachers

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:27.160
from remaining in the profession?

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:27.850
That's something that is--

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:29.580
JEB BUSH: Can I be blunt about this?

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:30.550
PAUL PETERSON: Yes.

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:32.020
JEB BUSH: You can fire them.

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:32.770
PAUL PETERSON: No.

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:35.860
JEB BUSH: Yes you can in
Florida, and a few other states.

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:39.580
Elimination of tenure was one
of the elements that we did.

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:43.420
And for teachers that have
been in the classroom,

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:47.140
they have to earn their
tenure by evaluations

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:51.520
that show that they're
satisfactory or excellent teachers.

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:58.000
There should be no teacher after
remediation that cannot deliver rising

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:01.530
student achievement in the classroom,
because it's so dangerous for students.

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:02.380
And at the same time there should be--

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:04.810
PAUL PETERSON: So how much remediation
do you think is appropriate?

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:05.770
Is it one year--

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:06.430
JEB BUSH: I think it's a year.

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:07.380
PAUL PETERSON: --give
them a probation year?

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:08.921
JEB BUSH: I think it probably varies.

00:06:08.921 --> 00:06:16.610
But the Florida system is three years of
performance data for student learning.

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:19.930
So you're taking an average over
three years, which I think is fair.

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:25.240
And then if they are below average,
significantly below average,

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:27.100
they can lose their tenure.

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:29.780
And all new teachers
have no tenure at all.

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:33.850
So the system needs to move
to a professional system

00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:37.390
not to a collective bargaining system
that's basically a trade union.

00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:39.670
Teachers I think are
deserving of higher pay.

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:42.310
And we're deserving,
families are, and our country

00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:44.570
is deserving of rising
student achievement.

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:46.570
So take Korea.

00:06:46.570 --> 00:06:50.260
Korea pays significantly
high teacher salaries.

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:53.790
And they finance it
by higher class sizes.

00:06:53.790 --> 00:06:57.310
In places in this country we think
class size is the determining factor

00:06:57.310 --> 00:06:59.000
for student achievement.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:01.309
No, it's quality teachers.

00:07:01.309 --> 00:07:04.600
PAUL PETERSON: So you're saying we could
get better teachers and better schools

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:09.850
if we didn't worry too much about
having extremely small classes?

00:07:09.850 --> 00:07:12.516
JEB BUSH: Class sizes,
there's no indication--

00:07:12.516 --> 00:07:14.890
I just read your research--
there's no indication that it

00:07:14.890 --> 00:07:17.200
yields rising student achievement.

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:21.790
But an effective teacher in front
of a student for a multiyear period

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:22.642
delivers.

00:07:22.642 --> 00:07:24.100
And that's what we should be doing.

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:27.910
I'm not saying let's just cram
students in classes for no purpose.

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:31.327
But wouldn't it be better to pay
teachers more for a job well done?

00:07:31.327 --> 00:07:33.160
And one of the ways you
could do that is not

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:37.760
have these mandated lower class sizes as
the norm in many parts of the country,

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:38.510
including Florida.

00:07:38.510 --> 00:07:40.593
PAUL PETERSON: But it's
hard to imagine that we're

00:07:40.593 --> 00:07:43.960
going to have a great educational
system without great teachers.

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:48.290
And so these ideas are ideas that we
have to do more than just talk about.

00:07:48.290 --> 00:07:49.950
We have to do something about them.

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:52.300
JEB BUSH: Yeah, and the
people, interestingly,

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:55.270
that oppose this generally
are the unions that

00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:58.100
represent teachers and
other school personnel,

00:07:58.100 --> 00:08:05.170
which is a tragedy because, in reality,
it's hard to get buy-in from the union

00:08:05.170 --> 00:08:05.800
leadership.

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:09.577
But teachers desperately want to
be recognized as professionals.

00:08:09.577 --> 00:08:11.410
And if they do extraordinary
work, they want

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:16.270
to be recognized not just in
terms of a pat on the back.

00:08:16.270 --> 00:08:21.070
What's wrong with providing them bonuses
and higher pay for the job they do?

00:08:21.070 --> 00:08:22.450
They have such a critical job.

00:08:22.450 --> 00:08:24.449
It is the one thing that
matters as it relates

00:08:24.449 --> 00:08:25.990
to whether a child is going to learn.

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:28.031
PAUL PETERSON: Well thank
you very much governor.

00:08:28.031 --> 00:08:29.760
Good to have you with us today.

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:31.410
JEB BUSH: Thanks Paul.

